# 01 — Variables, References & Const

## What is this?
C++ introduces true **references** (`Type&`) as transparent aliases to existing variables. `const` enforces immutability, while `constexpr` specifies compile-time evaluation.

## Why does C++ have it?
- References provide pass-by-reference semantics with clean syntax, avoiding pointer syntax (`*`, `&`) and null-pointer hazards.
- Passing large objects by `const Type&` eliminates expensive copy overhead while guaranteeing the original object cannot be mutated.

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```cpp
int x = 10;
int& ref = x; // ref is an alias to x
ref = 20;     // x is now 20

const int max_users = 100; // immutable
```

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.cpp`:
1. `void double_value(int& val)`: Doubles the value of `val` in place via reference.
2. `void swap_values(int& a, int& b)`: Swaps `a` and `b` using references.
3. `constexpr int compute_square(int n)`: Returns `n * n`.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```cpp
void double_value(int& val) {
    val *= 2;
}

void swap_values(int& a, int& b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

constexpr int compute_square(int n) {
    return n * n;
}
```
</details>

