# 03 — Strings & string_view

## What is this?
`std::string` manages heap-allocated dynamic text with automatic memory management. `std::string_view` (C++17) provides a zero-copy, read-only non-owning view over any character sequence.

## Why does C++ have it?
- Eliminates manual `malloc`, buffer overflows, and null terminator bugs common in C string manipulation.
- `std::string_view` avoids unnecessary string allocations when passing substrings or string literals into inspection functions.

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```cpp
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

std::string s = "Hello";
s += ", World!";

std::string_view sv = s; // Non-owning reference
```

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.cpp`:
1. `bool is_palindrome(std::string_view sv)`:
   - Returns `true` if characters read the same forwards and backwards, else `false`.
2. `std::string repeat_string(std::string_view sv, int times)`:
   - Returns a new `std::string` containing `sv` repeated `times` times. Returns empty string if `times <= 0`.
3. `int count_vowels(std::string_view sv)`:
   - Counts vowels (`a, e, i, o, u`, case-insensitive).

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <string_view>
#include <cctype>

bool is_palindrome(std::string_view sv) {
    if (sv.empty()) return true;
    size_t left = 0;
    size_t right = sv.size() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (sv[left] != sv[right]) return false;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return true;
}

std::string repeat_string(std::string_view sv, int times) {
    if (times <= 0) return "";
    std::string result;
    result.reserve(sv.size() * times);
    for (int i = 0; i < times; i++) {
        result += sv;
    }
    return result;
}

int count_vowels(std::string_view sv) {
    int count = 0;
    for (char ch : sv) {
        char lower = std::tolower(static_cast<unsigned char>(ch));
        if (lower == 'a' || lower == 'e' || lower == 'i' || lower == 'o' || lower == 'u') {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
