# 04 — STL Vectors & Iteration

## What is this?
`std::vector<T>` is the default sequence container in C++. It encapsulates a dynamically resizing contiguous array on the heap.

## Why does C++ have it?
- Guarantees O(1) random access via `operator[]` and amortized O(1) appending via `push_back`.
- Integrates with modern C++ range-based `for` loops (`for (const auto& item : vec)`).

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```cpp
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> nums = {1, 2, 3};
nums.push_back(4);
for (int n : nums) { /* ... */ }
```

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.cpp`:
1. `int sum_vector(const std::vector<int>& vec)`: Returns sum of all elements.
2. `std::vector<int> filter_even(const std::vector<int>& vec)`:
   - Returns a new vector containing only the even integers in `vec`.
3. `std::vector<int> reverse_vector(const std::vector<int>& vec)`:
   - Returns a new vector with elements in reverse order.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```cpp
#include <vector>

int sum_vector(const std::vector<int>& vec) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int n : vec) {
        total += n;
    }
    return total;
}

std::vector<int> filter_even(const std::vector<int>& vec) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    for (int n : vec) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) {
            result.push_back(n);
        }
    }
    return result;
}

std::vector<int> reverse_vector(const std::vector<int>& vec) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    result.reserve(vec.size());
    for (int i = static_cast<int>(vec.size()) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        result.push_back(vec[i]);
    }
    return result;
}
```
</details>

