# 06 — Destructors & RAII

## What is this?
Resource Acquisition Is Initialization (RAII) ties resource lifecycle to object lifetime. The constructor acquires resources, and the destructor (`~ClassName()`) automatically releases them when the object goes out of scope.

## Why does C++ have it?
- Eliminates resource leaks (memory, sockets, file descriptors, mutex locks) even when exceptions are thrown.

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```cpp
class FileGuard {
    FILE* file_;
public:
    FileGuard(FILE* f) : file_(f) {}
    ~FileGuard() { if (file_) fclose(file_); }
};
```

## What to Practice
In `exercise.cpp`, implement class `BufferGuard`:
- Allocates an array of `size` integers dynamically in heap memory (`new int[size]`).
- Stores pointer `data_` and `size_`.
- Destructor `~BufferGuard()` deallocates the array via `delete[] data_`.
- Methods:
  - `int get_size() const`
  - `void set(int index, int val)`
  - `int get(int index) const` (returns -1 if out of bounds)

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```cpp
class BufferGuard {
private:
    int* data_ = nullptr;
    int size_ = 0;
public:
    explicit BufferGuard(int size) : size_(size > 0 ? size : 0) {
        if (size_ > 0) {
            data_ = new int[size_]();
        }
    }

    ~BufferGuard() {
        delete[] data_;
        data_ = nullptr;
    }

    // Disable copying for RAII ownership simplicity
    BufferGuard(const BufferGuard&) = delete;
    BufferGuard& operator=(const BufferGuard&) = delete;

    int get_size() const { return size_; }

    void set(int index, int val) {
        if (index >= 0 && index < size_ && data_) {
            data_[index] = val;
        }
    }

    int get(int index) const {
        if (index >= 0 && index < size_ && data_) {
            return data_[index];
        }
        return -1;
    }
};
```
</details>

