# 08 — Inheritance & Virtual Functions

## What is this?
Dynamic polymorphism in C++ is achieved through inheritance and `virtual` member functions dispatched via virtual method tables (`vtable`).

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual ~Shape() = default;
    virtual double area() const = 0; // Pure virtual interface
};
```

## What to Practice
In `exercise.cpp`:
1. Base class `Shape`:
   - Virtual destructor `virtual ~Shape() = default;`
   - Pure virtual method `virtual double area() const = 0;`
2. Derived class `Circle : public Shape`:
   - Constructor `Circle(double radius)`
   - Override `double area() const override` (`3.14159 * radius * radius`)
3. Derived class `Square : public Shape`:
   - Constructor `Square(double side)`
   - Override `double area() const override` (`side * side`)

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual ~Shape() = default;
    virtual double area() const = 0;
};

class Circle : public Shape {
private:
    double radius_;
public:
    explicit Circle(double radius) : radius_(radius) {}
    double area() const override {
        return 3.14159 * radius_ * radius_;
    }
};

class Square : public Shape {
private:
    double side_;
public:
    explicit Square(double side) : side_(side) {}
    double area() const override {
        return side_ * side_;
    }
};
```
</details>

